# Implement explicit import and export

## Scope and existing evidence

Allow deliberate notebook bundle and `.atrament` profile reads or writes at
caller-selected or explicitly host-policy-admitted paths without converting them
into background persistence. Keep paths mentioned only in notebook, source,
diagnostic, clipboard, or model-response text as data rather than Export
authority.

Current export design evidence freezes accepted-revision binding, caller-owned
or explicit host-policy output intent, explicit path and overwrite behavior,
blocking validation, temporary cleanup, file-commit semantics, output identity,
same-retry lost-receipt recovery, external target drift handling, cancellation,
typed output results, concurrency, and browser/CLI/MCP parity.
Trusted host-caller Export admission, accepted session revision enforcement,
application integration and non-profile output execution remain open.

The pure profile serializer now produces complete, SHA-256-verified
`.atrament` ZIP artifact bytes, including
exact size planning and caller-owned memory emission. Those bytes are not bound
to an accepted session revision, authenticated selected-file target, overwrite
policy or durable commit.

A new outbound `atrament_profile_file_commit` primitive can write
already-admitted complete artifact bytes to an exact caller-supplied Linux
path with a strict byte ceiling. It resolves the parent directory through
symlink-forbidding `openat2` and stages complete 0600 bytes in an unnamed
`O_TMPFILE` inode. After file sync and the final cancellation check, a
preserve-existing request links directly to the selected name with atomic
no-overwrite; an explicit replacement creates a randomized sibling hard link
only for the final `renameat2`, then syncs the directory. A filesystem without
secure anonymous staging refuses rather than leaking partial files on crash.

The adapter refuses observed symlinks and nonregular replacement targets,
cleans any late-linked replacement staging path on ordinary refusal, and
distinguishes an uncertain post-link/rename sync failure from no commit.
Real-file regressions cover complete writes, conflicts, replacement, traversal,
links, byte ceilings, opaque names and concurrent preserve writers. Two
post-preflight replacement-swap tests now show that a symlink is replaced
without following its unrelated target, while a directory substituted just
before rename refuses and removes its late-linked staging name.

An opt-in `commit_profile_file_cancellable` variant now observes caller-owned
cancellation before any filesystem operation, between 64 KiB stage writes,
and after syncing the complete anonymous file, before linking a target.
`CancelledBeforeCommit` proves that no target link or rename occurred;
existing target bytes remain unchanged.

After linking or rename, cancellation is not polled and cannot revoke a
committed output. Four Linux cancellation fixtures cover early refusal,
mid-write cleanup, pre-commit replacement preservation and completed commits.
Two new SIGKILL-midwrite cases for preserve and replacement also show the
unnamed stage disappears without running destructors.

Replacement still has a narrow crash window after linking its temporary name
and before rename: that late named link can survive process death and needs
future safe recovery. The original non-cancellable file API delegates to the
same implementation.

This is only a
low-level file effect: it cannot establish trusted selection provenance,
accepted revision, artifact identity, retry recovery or the caller-owned
cancellation state. A trusted application must bind those before invocation; it
must not be wired directly to a model-supplied path. Non-Linux platforms
still fail closed.

The `atrament_profile_export_retry_binding` application now retains only a
bounded, process-local mapping from a caller retry key to one normalized
profile Export request. It binds accepted revision identity, exact opaque
selected path and source classification, explicit overwrite intent, full ZIP
and root SHA-256, and complete byte length. Reusing a key with different
inputs is a typed conflict, while a truly identical reuse is only
`SameBinding` and never evidence of a prior output effect.

The ledger refuses invalid keys, excessive selected path lengths and record
capacity exhaustion without replacing another binding. It never validates
caller provenance or current revision, acquires target locks, commits bytes,
tracks operation outcomes, or issues a receipt. Same-retry lost-receipt
recovery therefore still requires session-owned outcome evidence and target
drift verification; a key match cannot authorize blind Export replay.

The pure `atrament_profile_export_retry_recovery` composition now combines
retry binding disposition, session-owned file-commit outcome and a fresh
read-only target observation. Only an identical bound request with a previously
synced commit, matching byte count and matching full archive digest and length
is a candidate for prior-receipt recovery. New bindings, missing original
outcomes, known no-commit failures, uncertain post-rename durability, target
drift and failed or inconsistent inspection remain distinct non-success
states.

This classifier issues no receipt, stores no prior effect, performs
no I/O and never authorizes an Export retry. A trusted session application
must still retain the original commit outcome and receipt, authenticate the
caller and protect against concurrent target mutation. A real-file fixture
also refuses symlink substitution after a successful prior commit without
opening or modifying the linked file.

The read-only `atrament_profile_export_target_check` composition now compares
an independently classified `ExportProfile` path against a complete sealed
`ProfileExportArtifact`. It reuses secure no-symlink opening and a bounded,
exact-EOF file reader, then compares the complete byte sequences rather than
trusting only the ZIP filename or declared SHA-256. An absent path is `Missing`;
a different complete file, including a same-length valid ZIP replacement or
an oversized one, is `Drifted`.

Unsafe links, nonregular sources, unsupported platforms and unstable reads
refuse without asserting a match. Ten real-file checks include parity with
the atomic file writer and prove that comparison never rewrites targets.

The returned digest and length describe a matched artifact, **not** a prior
file-commit receipt:
it neither attests the host's selection gesture nor binds a session revision,
retry identity, output options, or historic operation. A matching current file
can still have been externally rewritten to identical bytes. Same-retry recovery
and drift-conflict reporting remain application responsibilities.

The `atrament_profile_export_session` composition now retains the original
file-commit outcome and a constructor-sealed receipt in bounded process-local
memory. A fresh retry key first binds revision, exact selected path, overwrite
and ZIP identity, then attempts the atomic Linux writer once. Only a successful
parent-directory-synced commit creates a receipt. Cancellation, existing-target
conflicts and other proven pre-commit refusals retain `KnownNoCommit`; uncertain
post-rename directory sync retains `DurabilityUnknown` without any receipt.

An identical repeated key never writes again. It returns the saved receipt
only after a new symlink-safe, complete-byte target check agrees with the
original sealed ZIP; changed revision, path, source, overwrite, archive or
root identity refuse before any I/O.

Missing, drifted, unsafe or substituted
files cannot be overwritten or counted as success by retry recovery. Dropping
the session loses the journal, not a deliberately exported file. Seven
real-file integration fixtures cover these states, key exhaustion, invalid
purpose, byte budget, and absent or symlink-swapped targets.

Receipts now retain both the original retry key and explicit overwrite
intent alongside the destination, accepted revision, archive digest and root
digest. Same-key recovery returns this original sealed receipt unchanged; it
does not silently derive replacement permission from current filesystem state.

Process-local Export retry bindings, the attempt journal, and saved receipts
now use redacted `Debug` output rather than exposing selected file paths,
retry keys or original content identifiers through routine error logging.
Their explicit typed accessors continue to return the exact original receipt
data to the owning application caller.

The bounded opened-file Import snapshot, complete in-memory Export artifact,
and borrowed Export payload inputs likewise redact private byte sequences and
archive paths from `Debug`. Existing typed byte access remains available to
the owning application caller; debug printing is not data access authority.

The stored ZIP reader now redacts original source bytes and path names in
its member, archive and paired-root `Debug` projections; its verified member
view additionally hides the referenced typed manifest. Exact CRC/SHA-verified
member access remains available through the explicit read-only methods.
The subsequent decoded-manifest and fully verified Import wrappers also redact
selection paths, profile identities and source payloads in `Debug` while
preserving explicit verified data access.

The source-backed JSON syntax views and their field, string and array
iterators now also redact borrowed private keys, values and raw JSON escapes
from `Debug` output. Explicit source-byte and decoded-text accessors remain
unchanged; plain and pretty debug regression fixtures cover every view.

Typed profile manifest values, entry declarations and their constructor-sealed
admission, inventory and verified-entry views now similarly redact private
profile identities, feature strings and archive paths in `Debug`. Their
explicit typed getters and diagnostic error values remain unchanged.

Manifest and archive-inventory refusal variants now also redact offending
feature identifiers, entry paths and version strings in implicit `Debug`.
They preserve the named error variants, safe reason codes and byte counts,
while exact offending text remains available through typed error fields.

A real `atrament profile inspect` subprocess regression now verifies that
rejected private duplicate features cannot leak through stderr, even when
the selected filename, section and asset contents also contain private
markers. The CLI still names the typed rejection and publishes no stdout.

All explicitly classified notebook/profile file-path intents now redact
the exact selected path from `Debug` while retaining source, purpose and
overwrite classification. Ordinary and non-UTF8 selected paths remain intact
through their typed accessors; no logging capability is inferred from intent.

Incremental SHA-256 state also redacts its pending private input block and
internal chaining words from `Debug`, while preserving byte counting and
streaming digest correctness across compression-block boundaries.

Source-backed entry reader failures now retain the original `io::Error`
for explicit typed inspection but redact its arbitrary error-message text
from `Debug`. Error kind, fixed resource limits and structural verification
reasons remain visible, without disclosing provider-supplied private paths.

Generic fallible chunk-verification failures now suppress arbitrary
upstream error payloads in `Debug` without imposing `E: Debug`, retaining
typed `Input(E)` ownership and visible structural `Verification` reasons.

Syntax-admitted JSON path selection now also redacts arbitrary decoded
member keys from `ProfileJsonPathSegment::Key` debug projections while
preserving exact typed lookup and visible numeric array indices.

Pre-serialization stored-ZIP member inputs now also redact caller-owned
private archive paths and raw payload bytes in `Debug`, matching the typed
Export payload and read-entry boundaries without altering ZIP serialization.

Real `atrament profile inspect` missing-source and selected-symlink
refusals now also assert error-type-only stderr: private selected path
markers, uninspected sections and assets never leak, and symlink targets
stay byte-for-byte unchanged.

A real-file cross-boundary regression also exports a canonical ZIP through
the disposable session and securely reopens that same selected path with the
existing strict Import verifier. It checks the decoded identity, complete
member verification and byte/inode stability without accepting a profile into
the session or granting any new file authority. The same fixture then
externally corrupts the ZIP and proves Import rejects it without exposing
verified members and same-key Export recovery reports drift without rewriting.

The journal reserves `NotRecorded` before invoking any cancellation callback
or staging I/O. If a callback unwinds, no saved outcome is fabricated, and the
same key refuses another write even if the selected file later holds identical
bytes. A real-file regression forces unwind after staging and file sync, checks
owned temporary cleanup and target preservation, then proves a distinct key
can still make a newly authorized attempt.

This is a trusted-runtime composition prerequisite, **not a public Export
command**: path provenance labels and a supplied revision identity do not
authenticate the caller or prove that the ZIP was derived from the currently
accepted profile. The session runtime must establish those facts before
calling it, enforce concurrency and lifecycle policy, and integrate UI, CLI
and MCP results. A target can still change after the read-only recovery
inspection, so the receipt describes the original synced effect, not a
permanent guarantee of current bytes. Non-Linux writes continue to refuse.

An outbound `atrament_profile_file_reader` now reads an already-opened
regular file with independent byte limits, initial cursor rewind and a
real EOF probe. The `atrament_profile_open_file_import` composition
checks classified `ImportProfile` intent before invoking that reader.
It then checks stored ZIP framing and CRC, bounded root JSON syntax,
typed manifest fields, inventory and every declared member SHA-256.

Fifteen tests use real temporary files, an unopened path-intent string,
an initial file cursor at EOF, a write-only handle, directories,
a Linux procfs file reporting zero length, budget boundaries,
CRC-correct equal-length payload substitution and malicious JSON. Wrong-purpose,
size, schema and digest failures never invoke inspection. The production
components perform no persistent writes or session state changes.

A two-payload file fixture proves that ZIP CRC-valid, equal-length edits
to either member fail its declared SHA-256 and expose neither member
through the all-or-nothing inspection callback.
Separate real-file regressions reject missing and undeclared member names,
as well as declared payload sizes above the Import-specific byte ceiling,
even when the ZIP framing, CRC and manifest JSON are otherwise valid.

A Linux-only `atrament_profile_selected_file_open` adapter now opens the
exact classified ImportProfile path with `openat2` anchored at the process
working directory or filesystem root. It refuses symlinks in every
component, parent traversal and nonregular files. The Import composition
still rejects wrong-purpose requests before file reading, and unsupported
kernels refuse without falling back to a racy `File::open`. Five
path-policy fixtures cover final and parent links, relative source,
pathname replacement after opening and invalid file types.

Two integration fixtures connect this opened descriptor to bounded
source reading, ZIP/root admission and every member SHA-256. The negative
fixture reconstructs a valid CRC ZIP with a same-length forged member;
the SHA mismatch refuses before any inspection callback is invoked.

The Import composition now applies one additional all-or-nothing gate after
manifest inventory and every declared member digest pass: each `sections/`
entry must contain an entire, resource-bounded JSON **object** with distinct
decoded keys. Independent per-section byte, nesting, key and value ceilings
are explicit in `ProfileOpenFileImportLimits`. A typed `SectionJson` refusal
reports the original manifest declaration index before **any** callback can
inspect a previously verified section. `assets/` remain opaque byte payloads.

Three real-file regressions exercise an authentic, correctly hashed malformed
section alongside an opaque binary asset, escaped duplicate JSON keys and
an over-limit valid object, plus a later invalid section after a valid one.
The Linux CLI now reuses the same gate, rather than rechecking section syntax
only inside its own inspection callback. Two additional real CLI regressions
prove that non-UTF-8 selected pathname bytes round-trip through OS argv and
that a valid early section cannot appear on stdout if a later section fails.

Independent real-file regressions further reject over-depth and over-member
sections, excess JSON values and invalid section UTF-8, without rejecting a
valid section merely because its opaque asset contains non-UTF-8 binary bytes.
A manifest-only archive passes even when all section limits are zero.

This proves section JSON syntax, not a versioned handwriting schema or admitted
session profile; both remain open.

The trusted host must still prove the `CallerSelection` or admitted policy
actually came from an authorized action, not document or model content;
a classified intent cannot authenticate its own origin. Non-Linux source
policy remains unsupported, and a concurrent same-length file rewrite
is not guaranteed to yield a stable disk snapshot. No adapter has accepted
a decoded handwriting profile into the current session.

The composition also tightens the ZIP reader's member-index budget to the
minimum of the ZIP total-member ceiling and the tighter of the
schema and Import non-root limits plus one root manifest slot.
A manifest-only ZIP is accepted at zero non-root entry allowance.
Excess counts refuse during ZIP framing before index allocation or
root decoding.

The composition now also offers a selected-path Import entrypoint that first
rejects wrong purpose, opens only the classified `intent.path()` through the
Linux secure-open adapter, reads the returned descriptor, then applies the
complete bounded ZIP/manifest/SHA gate. Unlike the injected-open-file path,
this entrypoint cannot inspect a different host-supplied descriptor while the
selected path is absent. Tests cover the exact chosen file, missing-path
substitution refusal, symlink refusal, wrong-purpose ordering and CRC-valid
SHA forgery. It still cannot prove a genuine user-selection gesture from an
untrusted source label; that must be established by a future host boundary.

Typed section decoding, explicit profile acceptance, revision-bound
Export application admission and retry recovery remain open. The low-level
atomic Linux file commit is implemented but is not yet caller-authorized or
session-bound.

Notebook/PDF Export still lacks an admitted renderer artifact, and profile
section encoders remain unfinished. Filesystem adapters
must not guess these missing ownership and authorization boundaries.

The shared derived/output result domain now freezes all 11 first-release core
result classes and their operation applicability across Render, Plan, and
Export.
It remains vocabulary only: no output operation, receipt normalization, retry
recovery, path handling, or file effect is implemented by that domain.

Its effect disposition separately distinguishes current-call read-only
projection, current-call file commit, recovered prior file commit, and known no
new effect without claiming any receipt or retry-store implementation.

A combined operation/result helper now exposes that disposition only for an
applicable Render, Plan, or Export pair, so callers cannot skip applicability
validation and accidentally project an operation-incompatible result.


Already-qualified lifecycle cancellation can now project into those same output
results without guessing execution state: proven pre-boundary cancellation maps
to Cancelled before result or effect; a crossed Render/Plan completion remains
Completed projection; and a crossed Export file commit remains Exported. A
request alone produces no final result. Cancellation signaling, adapter
execution, receipt normalization, and retry storage remain open.

## Explicit path-intent classification evidence

A transport-neutral `atrament_explicit_file_path_intent` domain now
classifies notebook and handwriting-profile imports and exports before any
file effect. It distinguishes caller selection and independently admitted
host policy from untrusted notebook/source, model, clipboard, and diagnostic
text; all content-derived sources are rejected as path intent.

The contract also rejects missing file-name components, disallows overwrite
requests for Import, preserves opaque non-UTF-8 selected paths, and retains
an explicit preserve-or-replace choice for Export. Integration fixtures cover
all 48 source/purpose/overwrite combinations plus invalid and opaque paths.

The classification is not a filesystem capability and does not check the
truthfulness of its claimed source. The future trusted caller or host adapter
must establish that source independently before invoking this contract and
still enforce path policy, symlink safety, serializer provenance, export
preflight, atomic commits, retry identity, and cancellation. No file has
been imported or exported by this work; the task remains open.

Autonomous Export intent now composes that source-classified path with the
existing autonomous goal-source gate. A 24-case fixture proves that model
text cannot authorize Export even if an independent path is selected, and
that an Import-class path cannot be substituted for an Export target. This
combination is still read-only and does not authorize a file effect.

## Read-only composite Export prerequisite evidence

An `atrament_export_prerequisite_projection` application now combines the
existing independent autonomous Export-source gate, exact Import-versus-Export
selected-path purpose, and revision-bound layout preflight. Its outcomes are
`NoExportIntent`, `WrongPathPurpose`, `BlockedLayout`, `IncompleteLayout`, and
`RequiresArtifactAndFileAdmission`: even the last outcome is **not** complete
Export readiness and cannot authorize a file effect. Source and path selection
remain claims that a future trusted transport must independently verify.

The composition is also reachable through the process-owned
`SessionApplication::preflight_explicit_export_prerequisites` method, which
performs the existing layout-revision binding against current accepted state
before projecting anything. Regression fixtures exercise the 72
source/purpose/layout combinations, genuine empty/incomplete layout evidence,
a genuine 6 mm fixed-region overflow, stale revision rejection after an
accepted semantic edit, and fresh-session refusal. Output artifacts, path
policy, serialization, atomic file commits, retries, and cancellation remain
unimplemented. No persistent output is created by this work.

The selected-file classifier now also rejects embedded NUL bytes before any
future file adapter can interpret the path, while retaining exact non-UTF-8
names and refusing directory-marker paths. Raw paths ending in `/.` or
`/..` now also reject before `Path::file_name()` can discard a trailing dot
component and mistake a directory selection for a file. The caller/host
selection provenance rule remains unchanged and no file effect is introduced.

## Explicit portable-profile Import preflight evidence

The new read-only `atrament_profile_import_preflight` application composes
trusted-source-classified `ImportProfile` file intent with canonical stored ZIP
encoding, admitted manifest features, and an exact declared-versus-observed
member-name inventory. Other import or export purposes reject **before** any
archive evidence is considered. Only after all gates pass does it return
borrowed, constructor-sealed evidence from which separately observed entry
lengths and SHA-256 digests may be checked before a future decoder sees bytes.

The Import fixture covers all 16 stored/compressed, extras, and ZIP64
combinations, rejected source/path purposes, unknown required features, missing
archive members, exact entry lookup without percent decoding, and length-before-
digest failure precedence. The caller must still prove the source provenance,
read and parse real archive bytes, establish measurements and hashes, validate
typed section schemas and resource bounds, and explicitly accept the resulting
profile into session state. Preflight success **does not** import a file,
persist anything, or claim an external path capability.

A mandatory, caller-selected Import resource budget now rejects excessive
manifest entry counts, oversized individual declared entries, aggregate declared
bytes, and `u64` sum overflow. The gate also caps observed archive-name counts
**before** allocating inventory membership sets. Tests cover exact bounds,
zero budgets, wrong-purpose precedence, and declared/observed limit failures.
These constraints operate on already-parsed declarations and name evidence:
actual decompressed byte counts, compression expansion, parser allocation,
media decoding and runtime memory ceilings must be enforced separately.

The separate in-memory `atrament_profile_zip_reader` now obtains ordinary
stored-only ZIP member names and byte slices from actual bounded ZIP framing,
including independently checked CRC-32. Its integration regression feeds
those observed names into this task's existing typed `ImportProfile` gate.
Actual filesystem admission, bounded file reading, manifest JSON decoding,
cryptographic digest recomputation, ZIP64, and user-controlled session profile
acceptance are still required before this TODO can close.

The separate profile-entry-sha256 primitive now provides exact SHA-256
computation and byte-length-first verification over admitted in-memory
member bytes. It has no file path, ZIP/JSON parsing, or accepted-profile
mutation. A future Import adapter must still establish genuine caller-owned
source authority, decode and validate the manifest, connect the archive's
exact byte slices to declarations, and explicitly commit an accepted profile.

The stored-ZIP inbound adapter now verifies **every** declared non-manifest
member against its exact bytes and SHA-256 after independently sealed
inventory preflight. It rechecks ZIP member names against that admitted
inventory before hashing, and returns borrowed entry bytes only after every
member succeeds. Cross-archive substitution, valid-CRC digest corruption,
and near-match entry lookup are regression-tested. The typed manifest still
needs to be parsed and bound to this ZIP's own root JSON; no file is opened
and no profile is accepted by this byte-level step.

A separate resource-bounded JSON syntax domain now validates one complete
UTF-8 root object and retains borrowed source bytes without allocating typed
values. The ZIP inbound adapter binds that syntax gate to the actual CRC-
checked `manifest.json` bytes and checks root byte, value and nesting limits.
The gate now rejects duplicate decoded keys in every object, even across
Unicode escapes and surrogate-pair aliases, using bounded fixed-size storage.
It still does not decode a typed manifest, establish the manifest's wire schema,
or authorize Import from any caller-selected filesystem path.

The constructor-sealed root JSON syntax result can now borrow a chosen
root-member value's exact byte slice from the checked ZIP manifest entry.
Lookup is Unicode-scalar exact and cannot turn nested fields or near-match
names into top-level values. The caller still cannot treat this generic byte
view as a typed import or a verified caller-authorized filesystem read.

Nested sealed JSON value views now distinguish six syntactic JSON kinds and
support exact-key object lookup, ordered array traversal and bounded indexed
array selection from one admitted ZIP manifest root. Structural selection is
not yet a typed profile manifest, a declared-entry digest proof, or authorized
filesystem Import; those checks remain mandatory before session mutation.

The JSON syntax evidence can now expose a decoded string scalar iterator and
non-coercing bool/null/plain `u64` helpers from an admitted ZIP root. The typed
manifest parser still must bind every required field to the actual root bytes,
refuse schema mismatches and unknown required features, and preserve optional
wire data before any Import/session effects.

Source-order sealed JSON member enumeration now retains original key spelling
and exact selected values without constructing a typed manifest. The future
codec must decide required versus optional keys and reject incompatible fields
before verified inventory, explicitly selected path and session Import effects.

Read-only sealed manifest JSON views now allow a caller-provided sequence of
object keys and array indices to select one original source-byte value, even
through nested ZIP-root structures. The actual typed manifest parser must
still decide allowed keys, enforce required/unknown field handling, bind
declared SHA-256 entries to this root, and pass explicit path authorization.

Decoded manifest JSON strings can now be copied without heap allocation into
bounded caller-owned storage, atomically refusing inadequate destination space
and non-string values. This does not permit using decoded values as paths,
selecting a source, or accepting a typed profile until the future import codec
and explicit filesystem authority establish those independent checks.

Sealed JSON member names now support bounded decoded UTF-8 copies without
allocating a JSON tree or reinterpreting input as path authority. Future typed
manifest schema recognition and unknown-field preservation still need their
own explicit policy before section decoding or session Import.

The stored-ZIP reader can now pair its own CRC-checked archive index and exact
syntax-admitted root JSON bytes in a sealed borrowed view. A future typed
manifest decoder can receive that association instead of independent arguments.
The typed schema, all declared SHA-256 entry checks, selected path authority,
and session acceptance are still unimplemented and cannot be inferred here.

Incremental SHA-256 evidence now supports future bounded streaming Import
without retaining an entire member as one allocation. The pure chunk verifier
checks declared byte length before comparing SHA-256 and does not open any file,
validate source path authority, or accept a profile. Actual I/O errors and
explicit path/source admission remain responsibilities of future adapters.

A fallible variant of the fixed-memory chunk verifier now preserves upstream
transport errors distinctly from short streams, declared length failures or
SHA-256 mismatches. The verifier consumes only caller-supplied in-memory
`Result` chunks; actual file access, interruption, authorization, cleanup and
profile acceptance remain unimplemented in the adapter layer.

Already SHA-256-verified stored ZIP payloads can now be accessed as borrowed,
nonzero-sized chunks, including distinct present-empty and absent cases.
Future section importers can consume those exact slices without whole-payload
copies; format decoding, source authorization, filesystem I/O and session
acceptance must still be implemented independently.

A bounded inbound reader-verification adapter now accepts only an independently
preopened byte source and a typed entry declaration. It refuses over-budget
declarations before I/O, hashes in fixed 8 KiB space and requires exact EOF
without buffering whole members. Original I/O errors stay distinct from a
short stream, excess bytes and digest mismatch. Trusted file opening, stream
timeouts, path authority and accepted-profile mutation remain future work.

Transport verification currently proves EOF only for the reader view supplied
by the trusted adapter, not necessarily the physical source beneath wrappers.
`Read::take` can hide trailing original bytes, so the eventual selected-file
reader must bind the complete original source scope and length independently.
The verifier explicitly tests and documents this distinction; it cannot serve
as proof of a selected source by itself.

Adversarial `Read` fixtures also reject impossible reported byte counts without
panicking and treat premature zero counts as EOF, not a way to retry hidden
bytes. The selected host transport must still ensure truthful Read semantics.

A separate pure application can produce deterministic stored-only ZIP framing
in memory from already chosen section bytes and an opaque supplied root.
It never serializes a typed manifest or writes a path; the missing canonical
JSON codec, explicit output artifact identity, selected destination authority,
atomic file commit and retry recovery still block genuine profile Export.

The pure stored-ZIP framing application can now preview exact final archive
size through the same failure checks used by its byte writer, without output
allocation or checksum work. This only supports eventual output resource
planning: the future host still needs source authenticity, typed manifest
serialization, explicit file intent and independent atomic commit authority.

Source-sealed ZIP root JSON also has a non-authorizing SHA-256 fingerprint
computed from exact CRC-checked, syntax-admitted root bytes. The fingerprint
is deliberately root-scoped: it does not establish full archive identity,
manifest semantic agreement, file authority or accepted Import state.

The checked archive now exposes a distinct SHA-256 of its complete original
ZIP source in addition to the root-only JSON SHA-256. Changed non-root bytes
alter only the archive-wide fingerprint; neither digest binds a selected file
handle, a trusted destination, a schema-valid manifest or Import acceptance.

Read-only source-backed ZIP framing evidence can now distinguish a readable
legacy-metadata archive from bytes matching the pure stored-ZIP writer. Even
when the complete framing matches, future Import must still admit canonical
JSON, a schema-typed root, declared entry hashes, chosen source authority and
session mutation independently; this indicator performs none of those steps.

The checked ZIP reader can now copy its exact archive path references into
caller-owned bounded storage with atomic too-small rejection. Future Import
inventory preflight can use this view instead of an infallibly allocated `Vec`
when memory policy requires it, but validated path names do not bind a typed
manifest or authorize a filesystem read.

The existing read-only explicit-profile Import preflight fixture now consumes
caller-owned borrowed ZIP paths directly, including its missing-root failure.
No additional observed-name vector is needed for that adapter integration;
selected source authority, root schema decoding, payload digest acceptance and
session effects remain independently unimplemented.

An opt-in sorted Import preflight now shares the existing path-purpose,
encoding, budget and typed-manifest gates while avoiding additional inventory
membership-set allocations on success. It rechecks ZIP-order claims, returns
typed order/count errors and seals the same inventory as general preflight.
An 8,192-case sorted mutation corpus and a 65,534-name ZIP32 boundary guard
acceptance equivalence; the real CRC-checked ZIP fixture checks both paths.
No typed manifest is constructed from root bytes, and no file is imported.

A read-only CRC-correct ZIP regression now exercises sorted Import preflight
through complete entry SHA-256 checks, preserving exact borrowed source bytes.
An altered member with a valid new CRC and unchanged inventory still passes
structural preflight but fails declared SHA-256, even though root syntax and
its exact-source fingerprint remain unchanged. No profile is accepted.

An exhaustive 128-case comparison also proves that both Import preflight
variants return identical earlier path-purpose, archive-encoding and budget
results across all first-release ZIP metadata combinations. Only the final
inventory gate differs by explicit ordering and count diagnostics.

The CRC-checked stored ZIP reader now projects its own archive encoding facts
for Import preflight, replacing synthetic encoding values in integration tests.
This observation is sourced from checked bytes, including legacy ZIP headers,
but still has no filesystem path authority or typed manifest-root provenance.

The pure stored-ZIP encoder can now fill already caller-owned, explicitly
bounded memory without allocating another archive-sized output vector. Exact
size preflight occurs before any mutation, with atomic too-small refusal and
unchanged surplus bytes. This remains byte-only framing, not a canonical
manifest serializer, authorized Export target or atomic filesystem commit.

The read-only Export-byte/Import-byte boundary test now connects caller-owned
ZIP writer output to CRC-checked reading, source-backed encoding observations,
bounded borrowed path views, sorted typed inventory and complete SHA-256 entry
verification. A ten-case matrix additionally proves that both writers and
planning refuse the same malformed or over-budget inputs before output writes.

A new strict typed manifest JSON decoder consumes only already syntax-admitted
root evidence. It enforces exact required object fields, lowercase SHA-256 hex,
plain `u64` lengths, and caller-chosen string, feature and entry ceilings;
unknown wire keys refuse instead of disappearing. A real ZIP fixture now
composes source-backed root decoding with sorted Import preflight and full
member digest verification, including a CRC-correct same-length mutation.
This advances read-only prerequisites but does not open a selected file,
authorize Import or Export, decode handwriting sections or accept a profile.

The typed root decoder also now offers a constructor-sealed result pairing the
manifest to its exact checked archive and root SHA-256 fingerprint. This removes
a detached-manifest substitution opportunity for future Import composition.
It still requires explicit caller-selected file evidence, inventory and entry
hash checks, typed sections and session acceptance before completing Import.

The new read-only `atrament_profile_import_verification` application now
accepts only a source-sealed decoded ZIP manifest and caller-classified
`ImportProfile` intent, copies the ZIP's actual sorted member names into
caller-owned slots, admits the typed declaration and resource budget, then
checks **every** declared payload's length and SHA-256 before exposing borrowed
member bytes. The result ties the same archive and root digest to its verified
manifest instead of allowing a caller to pair unrelated values. Seven fixtures
cover success, wrong-purpose priority, atomic undersized-slot refusal,
required-feature and resource limits, undeclared members, CRC-correct changed
bytes, and byte-length-before-digest precedence. A trusted selected-file
adapter, actual source provenance, typed section decoding, and explicit
session-state acceptance remain open; no filesystem effect occurs here.

Two further source-bound fixtures use a profile with two distinct member
payloads and different declaration versus ZIP ordering. Both must verify
before either is exposed; changing either payload while regenerating its ZIP
CRC still refuses the whole result at the correct declaration index. A valid
first member cannot leak as partially verified Import evidence when the
second member fails its hash.

The source-bound verifier also admits an exact manifest-only ZIP with an
empty declaration inventory and a zero-byte, zero-entry payload budget. Its
sealed result exposes no member payload, including `manifest.json`. This
proves empty profiles do not require artificial payload allocations.

## In-memory complete profile Export artifact evidence

The new `atrament_profile_export_assembly` application combines validated
canonical manifest JSON, an exact caller-supplied payload set and stored-only
ZIP framing into one owned artifact. It sorts paths without normalization,
requires a one-to-one declared/supplied inventory and verifies every member's
byte length before comparing its SHA-256 digest. Only a fully admitted set can
produce a complete archive, its root fingerprint and its archive fingerprint.

An integration fixture decodes the resulting ZIP root and verifies every
member through the independent source-attached Import path. Negative fixtures
exercise missing and unexpected members, duplicate or divergent names,
early/late digest mismatch, length-before-digest precedence and resource caps.
A generated 256-case corpus proves canonical byte identity under manifest and
payload reordering and rejects corrupted members without partial artifacts.

This is a pure **in-memory** profile artifact, not an Export command. A trusted
caller-selected path adapter, durable atomic commit, overwrite/retry handling,
section encoders and session lifecycle binding still need implementation.

The complete profile artifact assembly now also provides exact ZIP byte-count
planning and caller-owned ZIP emission using the same typed-manifest, inventory,
length, digest and ZIP32 gates as owned assembly. A short buffer is refused
before any destination mutation; surplus capacity is untouched and the returned
root/full-archive SHA-256 fingerprints cover only emitted source bytes.

Two additional fixtures prove atomic refusal of wrong bytes, resource limits
and undersized caller buffers. The 256-case canonical corpus now compares all
three paths byte-for-byte and confirms that even later corrupted payloads
cannot mutate an already owned destination. The writer still does not establish
selected destination authority or an atomic filesystem commit.

The assembly gate now runs the stored-ZIP count, declared byte-budget and ZIP32
preflight **before** SHA-256 work on potentially large supplied payload bodies.
Exact path inventory is still matched first, and admitted archives retain their
length-before-digest failure priority. A focused three-writer regression
confirms
that an unaffordable archive fails by typed ZIP budget rather than hashing
corrupt member bytes; a sufficient budget still detects the digest mismatch.
No output or caller-owned buffer is affected by these refusals.

A Linux full-member Export session integration now commits a stored profile
with one syntax-valid JSON section and one opaque binary asset. The separate
secure selected-file Import verifier independently authenticates both exact
member bytes and archive identity from that committed file. Replacing it
externally with another valid same-size archive changes the admitted section
but cannot turn same-key Export recovery into a receipt or another write.
